{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cox's Theorem.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cox's Theorem.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cox s theorem cox s theorem","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cox's Theorem.md","fileName":"Cox's Theorem.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cox's Theorem","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 21:47\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Probability Interpretation and Uncertainty]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Probability Interpretation and Uncertainty","bayesianprogramming.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cox's Theorem.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cox's Theorem.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cox s theorem cox s theorem","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cox's Theorem.md","fileName":"Cox's Theorem.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cox's Theorem","heading":"Cox's Theorem","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Cox's theorem derives the probability rules from requirements for consistent reasoning about plausibility. Plausibility is represented numerically, equivalent ways of calculating the same claim must agree, and changes in support for a proposition must relate consistently to its negation and conjunctions.\n\nUnder these requirements, plausible reasoning follows normalization and the conjunction rule. A calculus that violates those rules contradicts at least one of the stated consistency requirements.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Probability Interpretation and Uncertainty","bayesianprogramming.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cox's Theorem.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cox's Theorem.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cox s theorem cox s theorem","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cox's Theorem.md","fileName":"Cox's Theor